52
00:07:14.000 --> 00:07:17.160
Tim Nunamaker: Okay, so this is basically…

53
00:07:17.300 --> 00:07:19.810
Tim Nunamaker: The life cycle of a data connector.

54
00:07:20.330 --> 00:07:26.200
Tim Nunamaker: And you can think of a data connector as running this Stateful Execution protocol.

55
00:07:26.480 --> 00:07:33.000
Tim Nunamaker: At a very basic level, all a data connector is doing is collecting data, and that's this big green circle.

56
00:07:33.550 --> 00:07:36.129
Tim Nunamaker: So we need to start that process.

57
00:07:36.260 --> 00:07:43.219
Tim Nunamaker: with some initialization, then we need to end it, then we can admit that we are done collecting data. And then…

58
00:07:43.350 --> 00:07:52.999
Tim Nunamaker: in between, or during this process, we may need to bring a human in the loop, or we may need to request some information. So, for example, if

59
00:07:53.330 --> 00:07:57.160
Tim Nunamaker: I have credentials that I don't want to provide directly

60
00:07:57.310 --> 00:08:03.149
Tim Nunamaker: To the runtime, or to the data connector, and save those on disk, or something like that.

61
00:08:03.420 --> 00:08:18.229
Tim Nunamaker: They can be requested during the collection process. Maybe the runtime will mediate that by notifying the user, hey, this data connector needs a passkey, or some kind of manual approval or interaction.

62
00:08:18.440 --> 00:08:26.530
Tim Nunamaker: Or maybe the human delegates that to some other process that they have, but there's… A handoff.

63
00:08:26.790 --> 00:08:30.619
Tim Nunamaker: over… Like, an interaction, which can be scripted.

64
00:08:31.020 --> 00:08:35.949
Tim Nunamaker: And then if that interaction is fulfilled, then the data connector can go back to collecting data.

65
00:08:36.299 --> 00:08:40.659
Tim Nunamaker: Of course, data connectors can fail, and then there are different kinds of

66
00:08:41.090 --> 00:08:45.600
Tim Nunamaker: Like, error codes and information that can be emitted throughout the process.

67
00:08:46.070 --> 00:09:02.949
Tim Nunamaker: And I also don't want to undersell the initialization piece of this. The runtime has an important job of making sure that the data connector actually has what it needs. It validates that the data connector has a PDP-conformant manifest, and

